  "answer_text": "Great question! Here's a detailed explanation of why nitrogen is used in plastic welding:\n\n## Why Nitrogen is Used in Plastic Welding\n\n### 1. \ud83d\udee1\ufe0f It Acts as a Shielding Gas\nNitrogen gas is used when plastic welding because it acts as a shielding gas, protecting the weld area from contaminants in the air. This is much like shielding gases used when welding steel or aluminum with a MIG welder, to prevent the plastic from oxidizing during the welding process.\n\n### 2. \u2697\ufe0f It Prevents Oxidation\nAir is composed of 80% nitrogen and 20% oxygen, and the oxygen in the air causes oxidation, burning the plastic during the welding process. This results in smoke, ash, and sparks, which weaken the weld and create contamination. Welding with nitrogen eliminates this issue by flushing out the oxygen from the weld zone \u2014 without oxygen, the plastic melts cleanly without burning or oxidizing.\n\n### 3. \ud83d\udcaa It Produces a Stronger, Cleaner Weld\nA nitrogen hot air welder uses compressed nitrogen gas to eliminate oxygen from the weld area. The nitrogen acts as a shielding gas and allows for a contaminant-free weld with less smoke, which creates a stronger weld. This allows the melted plastics to fuse together with no contamination for a stronger, more durable weld.\n\n### 4. \ud83c\udf21\ufe0f It Has Beneficial Thermodynamic Properties\nNitrogen also has thermodynamic properties that make it useful when welding plastics like ABS and polypropylene; its high heat capacity allows for faster cooling of the welded material without causing warping or cracking due to thermal shock.\n\n### 5. \ud83d\ude97 It's Especially Important for Automotive Plastics\nThis type of weld can only be done on thermoplastics. Thermoplastics, such as polypropylene/thermoplastic polyolefin (PP/TPO), which is used to make most bumper covers today, work very well with this type of welder.\n\n### Hot Air vs. Nitrogen \u2014 The Key Difference\nThe problem with regular hot air welding lies in the oxygen \u2014 when you weld with regular hot air, the oxygen causes oxidation, which weakens the weld and makes it more likely to crack down the road. The nitrogen shielding gas prevents oxidation, ensuring the strongest welds possible, meaning you get a solid, long-lasting weld that stands up to vibration, cold, and heat.\n\nIn summary, nitrogen's **inert (non-reactive) nature** is the key \u2014 it displaces oxygen at the weld site, preventing burning and oxidation, and results in a much cleaner, stronger, and more durable plastic weld compared to using regular hot air.",
